s progress?】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # Literacy Routines: Growing a family of readers\nL19: \nL20:   * February 20, 2026 22:22\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: ## Why are literacy routines important?\nL23: \nL24: Just like brushing teeth and picking up toys, you want literacy activities to be part of your child’s daily routine. However, building a joy and love of reading requires a slightly different approach than reminding your child to do their chores.\nL25: \nL26: Studies have shown that children who have a strong literacy routine at home are more likely to read for pleasure and read above grade level later in life.\nL27: ## How can I help my family create literacy routines?\nL28: \nL29: A few simple steps can go a long way in helping your whole family find joy in reading.\nL30: \nL31: Here are some suggestions and tips to get you started. You can 【13†download】 this tip sheet below.\nL32: \nL33: 【14†Image: KhanKids_LiteracyRoutines.png】\nL34: ## How can the Khan Kids app help build literacy routines?\nL35: \nL36: The 【15†Khan Academy Kids†learn.khanacademy.org】 app has thousands of standards-aligned activities that are designed to be fun, joyful, and help young children develop literacy skills. We also have over 300 books in our library to keep your learner engaged and excited to read more. 【16†Regular use of our app has been shown to significantly increase pre-literacy skills】 and is a valuable resource to use with your child.\nL37: However, as much as we love our app, it can never replace the impact that reading together will have on your little one. We provide the practice activities for your child as they become an independent reader, but your family literacy routines pave the way for them to be a lifelong learner.\nL38: ## Additional articles\nL39: \nL40: This is article #5 in our series of 5 articles about the path to reading. If you’d like to learn more, be sure to read the other articles!\nL41: \nL42: Article 1) 【17†Pre-literacy】: the period of time before a child begins to read or write\nL43: \nL44: Article 2) 【18†Phonics】: the connection between letters and sounds\nL45: \nL46: Article 3) 【19†Fluency】: reading naturally, while paying attention to meaning\nL47: \nL48: Article 4) 【20†Comprehension】: understanding and thinking about what you read\nL49: ## 👇 Download our literacy routines tip sheet\nL50: \nL51:   * 【21†What can I do to create literacy routines for my family?】\nL52: \nL53: 500 KB 【21†Download】\nL54: \nL55: Was this article helpful?\nL56: \nL57: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL58: \nL59: 3 out of 3 found this helpful\nL60: \nL61: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL62: \nL63: 【22†Return to top】\nL64: \nL65: ## Comments\nL66: \nL67: 0 comments\nL68: \nL69: Article is closed for comments.\nL70: \nL71: 【23†Khan Academy】\nL72: \nL73: 【24†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPre-literacy: Setting your child up for success – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4403213371661-Pre-literacy-Setting-your-child-up-for-success)\nciteturn1025view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1023view0\",\"id\":13}); Total lines: 109\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Our Accessibility Statement】\nL8:   * 【3†Get in touch!】\nL9:   * 【4†Welcome to Khan Academy Kids】\nL10:   * 【5†Parent and Home Account Users: Getting Started and creating an account with Khan Academy Kids】\nL11:   * 【6†How do I set up an account?】\nL12:   * 【7†How do I verify my email?】\nL13:   * 【8†List of resources for parents】\nL14:   * 【9†Printable activities for parents and teachers】\nL15:   * 【10†Weekly Planners for Ages 2-8】\nL16:   * 【11†How does the learning level adjust and how do I view my child's progress?】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # Pre-literacy: Setting your child up for success\nL19: \nL20:   * February 26, 2026 23:51\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: \nL23: ## What is pre-literacy?\nL24: \nL25: Pre-literacy is the period of time before a child can read or write on their own. Children start developing pre-literacy skills as soon as they are born, and there is a lot of research that shows a strong pre-literacy foundation will lead to long-term success both in and out of school. Pre-literacy can include:\nL26: ### Narrative skills\nL27: \nL28:   * Telling or understanding stories\nL29:   * Asking and answering questions\nL30:   * Participating in conversations and following directions\nL31: \nL32: ### Vocabulary development\nL33: \nL34:   * Knowing and using all kinds of words\nL35:   * Asking questions about new words\nL36:   * Making connections between words: for example, comparing the words “big” and “huge” or connecting the word “jump” to the meaning of the word “jumpy”\nL37: ### Print awareness\nL38: \nL39:   * Knowing what printed words are and noticing them\nL40:   * Understanding how to hold and handle a book\nL41:   * Following along as a book is read; knowing that English print is read from left to right\nL42: \nL43: ### Print motivation\nL44: \nL45:   * Being excited to read and listen to stories\nL46:   * Thinking about and responding to stories\nL47:   * Seeing reading as something fun\nL48: ### Letter knowledge\nL49: \nL50:   * Knowing that there are different letters that make different sounds\nL51:   * Knowing the names of the letters\nL52:   * Knowing the sounds the letters make\nL53: ## What can I do to help my child develop pre-literacy skills?\nL54: \nL55: Setting your child up for success as a reader and writer is easier than you might think. As long as you're reading, talking, and listening as a family, you are helping your child grow their pre-literacy skills. Here are some ideas to get you started. You can 【13†download】 this tip sheet below.\nL56: \nL57: 【14†Image: KhanKids_PreLiteracy.png】\nL58: ## How does the Khan Kids app support pre-literacy skills?\nL59: \nL60: Great question! The 【15†Khan Academy Kids†learn.khanacademy.org】 app has activities for children as young as 2 years old and includes lots of pre-literacy practice.\nL61: In the Library, make sure you’ve set the dropdown menu to selected Kindergarten or Pre-K. In the Videos tab, check out the Early Reading and Language collection. This will give you some ideas for how to talk about and model pre-literacy skills for your learner. This 【16†video of Reya†youtu.be】 talking about the parts of a book is a good example of how Khan Kids reinforces the pre-literacy skills your child is developing.\nL62: \nL63: \nL64: \nL65: 【17†iframe†www.youtube-nocookie.com】\nL66: \nL67: Other activities in the app include:\nL68:   * Sing along with songs by our partner Super Simple, located in the Videos tab\nL69:   * Encourage your child to record a story to go along with their art in the Create tab\nL70:   * Listen to stories read aloud in the Reading tab, and talk about the questions together as they come up\nL71:   * Explore letters and sounds in the Letters tab; try starting with the letters in your child’s name\nL72: No matter what, as long as you’re reading, talking, and having fun, your child is well on their way to developing a strong foundation for literacy.\nL73: ## Additional articles\nL74: \nL75: This is article #1 in our series of 5 articles about the path to reading. If you’d like to learn more, be sure to read the other articles!\nL76: \nL77: Article 2) 【18†Phonics】: the connection between letters and sounds\nL78: \nL79: Article 3) 【19†Fluency】: reading naturally, while paying attention to meaning\nL80: \nL81: Article 4) 【20†Comprehension】: understanding and thinking about what you read\nL82: \nL83: Article 5) 【21†Reading routines】: developing lifelong readers\nL84: ## 👇 Download our pre-literacy tip sheet\nL85: \nL86:   * 【22†KhanKids_PreLiteracy.pdf】\nL87: \nL88: 500 KB 【22†Download】\nL89: \nL90: Was this article helpful?\nL91: \nL92: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL93: \nL94: 14 out of 16 found this helpful\nL95: \nL96: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL97: \nL98: 【23†Return to top】\nL99: \nL100: ## Comments\nL101: \nL102: 0 comments\nL103: \nL104: Article is closed for comments.\nL105: \nL106: 【24†Khan Academy】\nL107: \nL108: 【25†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll about Teacher Tools in Khan Academy Kids – Khan Academy (https://khankids.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/360041862972-All-about-Teacher-Tools-in-Khan-Academy-Kids)\nciteturn1025view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1023view1\",\"id\":3}); Total lines: 80\nL0: 【0†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Submit a request】\nL3: \nL4: [Input][Input] [Input: Search]\nL5: \nL6: Articles in this section\nL7:   * 【2†Our Accessibility Statement】\nL8:   * 【3†Five ways to use Khan Kids for back to school success】\nL9:   * 【4†Quick start guide for preschool through 2nd grade teachers】\nL10:   * 【5†✨ NEW: Teacher Dashboard on the web】\nL11:   * 【6†✨ NEW: Manage student access to areas of the app】\nL12:   * 【7†Module 1: Setting up a Class Account】\nL13:   * 【8†Learning Topics: Using Khan Academy Kids in Educational Settings】\nL14:   * 【9†Module 2: Using Class Codes】\nL15:   * 【10†Daily Schedules for Ages 2-8】\nL16:   * 【11†Module 3: Assigning lessons】\nL17: 【12†See more】\nL18: # All about Teacher Tools in Khan Academy Kids\nL19: \nL20:   * February 26, 2026 23:34\nL21:   * Updated\nL22: \nL23: 【13†Image: Photo1_Banner.png】\nL24: 【14†Khan Academy Kids†learn.khanacademy.org】 has Teacher Tools to supercharge learning for Pre-K through 2nd grade students! To access these features, simply 【14†download our app†learn.khanacademy.org】 and set up a Class Account. The app is 100% free and can be downloaded from the 【15†Apple†apps.apple.com】, 【16†Google Play†play.google.com】, or 【17†Amazon†www.amazon.com】 app store. You can also find us in the 【18†Clever app library†clever.com】.\nL25: If you already have a Khan Kids account but are not seeing any of the features mentioned in this article, you may need to 【19†convert your account】 in the Parent Section.\nL26: Khan Academy Kids engages Pre-K—2nd grade students in a learning journey designed in collaboration with learning experts at Stanford University, and aligned with the Common Core State Standards. Students love the engaging, interactive, and fun learning experience, quickly endearing themselves to the five characters and their diverse interests. With the help of the guide character Kodi bear, students can navigate the app completely independently.\nL27: And rest assured, everywhere students tap leads to an educational experience.\nL28: ## See what teachers can do with Khan Kids!\nL29: \nL30:   * 【20†Easily set up your class】\nL31:   * 【21†Search for lessons by standard】\nL32:   * 【22†Create assignments to reinforce key skills that are being taught in class】\nL33:   * 【23†Monitor student progress with in-depth reports】\nL34: ### Easily set up your class\nL35: \nL36: Set up all the students in your class quickly and easily. After creating your class, you’ll have a unique Class Code, which you can find in your Teacher Settings. Students can use this Class Code to sign in to your class in seconds.\nL37: You can edit student information from your teacher view in the Khan Kids app. Go to the Students tab and select a student name to 【24†edit a student’s name, avatar, or their learning level】. The level you select will serve as a starting point for their personalized Learning Path, which will adapt to meet their skill level for different topics.\nL38: When students sign in for the first time, all they have to do is press the big play button to begin their learning journey! Learn more about the Learning Path 【25†here】.\nL39: Does your school use Clever? Khan Kids supports automatic rostering for teachers who have a Clever account. Learn more 【26†here】.\nL40: \nL41: 【27†Image: StudentTab.png】\nL42: ### Search for lessons by standard\nL43: \nL44: Our content is mapped to the Head Start Early Learning Outcomes Framework and Common Core State Standards. This will make it easy for you to select lessons that fit with your teaching plans. Use the dropdown menu to search for the standard you'd like to address, and we'll pull up a selection of lessons for you to assign to your class. We show you how 【28†here】.\nL45: \nL46: 【29†Image: Assign_StandardSearch_copy.png】\nL47: ### Create assignments\nL48: Teacher Tools allow teachers to quickly find lessons to reinforce key skills that are being taught in class. As you browse through the tabs at the top of the screen—Letters, Reading, Math, Logic+, Books, and Videos—you’ll find lessons for each letter of the alphabet, for numbers 1-20+, for patterning, sight words, phonemes, shapes, word problems, and so much more. Teachers can tap any lesson to assign.\nL49: When the assignment has been created, students will see a heart icon that indicates new assignments are waiting for them!\nL50: And now, differentiation is easier than ever—teachers can assign lessons to the whole class, to an individual student, or to a small group. Lessons are clearly labeled according to their level: Main, Practice 1, Practice 2, and Basic. Start by assigning the Main lesson, and then assign the Basic version to students who need extra support, and Practice lessons to students who are ready for a challenge. Learn more about assigning lessons 【30†here】.\nL51: \nL52: 【31†Image: AssignDialog.png】\nL53: ### Monitor student progress with in-depth reports\nL54: \nL55: Our 【32†progress reports】 are newly refreshed to help you meet students at their level. We’ve added new information so you can identify student needs at a glance, and we’ve added new features that make it easy for you to respond to this information in real time. Find these reports in your teacher view by selecting the Students tab and tapping Class Reports.\nL56: You’ll see two different reports: an Assignments report and an All Progress report. The Assignments report only shows lessons that you or your co-teacher have assigned. The All Progress report shows every lesson that students have completed. This includes their completed assignments, activities they've completed in their Learning Path, and any lessons they've played through in the Library. The All Progress report combines this information so you can see students' overall progress to learning domain\nL57: Tap a lesson on the left side of the screen to assign, edit, or preview. Tap a student score to view each past attempt and the corresponding score history. You can also now email these reports to your desktop computer for easy viewing, or email individual student reports to parents for an academic update. Learn more about these reports 【32†here】.\nL58: \nL59: 【33†Image: read_edited_copy.png】  | 【34†Image: ClassReport.png】\nL60: \nL61: \nL62: \nL63: Was this article helpful?\nL64: \nL65: [Button: Yes] [Button: No]\nL66: \nL67: 36 out of 38 found this helpful\nL68: Have more questions? 【1†Submit a request】\nL69: \nL70: 【35†Return to top】\nL71: ## Comments\nL72: \nL73: 0 comments\nL74: \nL75: Article is closed for comments.\nL76: \nL77: 【36†Khan Academy】\nL78: \nL79: 【37†Powered by Zendesk†www.zendesk.com】\n"
